# Plan — etude-n9b.2 checkout isolation and seat prompt

Goal: consume the gated `read_checkout` contract so model seats are output-only
by default and receive reproducible read access only after an explicit workflow
grant, while placing the measurement-authority rule in the exact prompt every
seat reads.

ANCESTOR: etude-n9b (P1) — deliver GitHub issue #16 as an opt-in checkout-read
verification capability without changing the default. MEASUREMENT: today both
`GateStage` and live `runGate` call `runGateSeats` with the checkout as every
seat's process working directory; there is no neutral output-only execution path.

BLAST RADIUS: none: worktree and repo only. Tests create and remove private temp
git repositories/worktrees. No host package, system path, container, shared
service, credential, or external state is changed. Gate scratch stays bounded
to one per-seat neutral directory and is removed with the existing scratch root.

## Build decision

Build. The contract child establishes a per-gate boolean; this child is the
consumer that makes the grant real. Existing `replay.ExecRunner` already treats
its `WorktreeDir` as the child process cwd, so selecting a neutral sibling
directory for false and the pinned checkout for true uses the existing primitive.

## Runtime design

1. Thread `gate.ReadCheckout` through both gate paths into `runGateSeats` and
   `buildGatePrompt`.
2. For `read_checkout: false` or an absent field, create a unique empty cwd for
   each seat under the system-temp gate scratch root. The existing runner
   contract rejects scratch at or below the checkout; add an explicit relative-
   path assertion before launch so the neutral cwd is provably outside the
   checkout. The seat still receives only the shared prompt through
   `ETUDE_INPUTS_DIR`; it has no checkout as cwd. A unique cwd also avoids
   cross-seat hidden state, independently of the trusted registry mode.
3. For `read_checkout: true`, use the pinned checkout as the seat cwd. Live runs
   already hold the detached run checkout. A supervised `etude gate` normally
   reviews uncommitted work in the caller tree, which cannot be reproduced from
   the captured commit. Therefore a supervised read grant first requires the
   caller tree to be clean; a dirty tree is a clear fail-closed error before any
   seat runs. On a clean tree, materialize a fresh detached checkout at the
   captured reviewed stage's `git_sha`; checks continue to run in the caller
   tree. Fail closed if the pin cannot be materialized, always call the existing
   worktree cleanup (which removes and prunes registration), and tell the seat
   the exact access mode and pin in its prompt. This preserves the supervised
   default: ordinary dirty pre-commit gates remain usable only as output-only.
   Until GitHub issue #14 lands, also fail closed when the pinned tree contains
   `.gitmodules`: an explicit grant must never expose an empty/unidentified
   submodule directory. This guard is replaced by #14's populated-and-recorded
   submodule proof after rebase, not silently bypassed.
4. Record the resolved boolean on every `GateAttempt` created by either gate
   path. False stays omitted from JSON by the schema contract; true is durable.
5. Build one identical prompt for all seats. It states the resolved access mode
   and carries this rule verbatim in substance:
   - embedded provenance is AUTHORITATIVE for measurements;
   - seats must not re-derive arithmetic because provenance is embedded so they
     do not;
   - checkout reads exist to falsify a stage-output claim, not recompute it;
   - when checkout evidence shows the output wrong, return BLOCK with evidence
     instead of silently substituting new numbers.

The prompt carries the rule even on output-only gates, phrased conditionally for
the unavailable checkout, so no seat invocation depends on prose outside the
prompt for measurement authority.

## Complete call-site set

- `Engine.GateStage` in `internal/liverun/gate_once.go` (supervised gate).
- `Engine.runGate` in `internal/liverun/gate.go` (live run and forward replay).
